  "query_preview": "..\nToday: The shortwave that brought showers to the region yesterday \nhas moved out of the area. However, lingering instability with CAPE \nvalues 200-500 J/kg will bring a chance at continued afternoon and \nevening thunderstorms. The east slopes of the Cascades have the \nlargest chance (20 to 30 percent) at thunderstorms. Main impacts \nwith these thunderstorms will be isolated heavy downpours, fire \nstarts due to lightning, and outflow wind gusts up to 30 mph due to \n500-600 J/kg of DCAPE. Isolated heavy downpours especially in steep \nterrain and near burn scars bring the chance of debris flows. As \nsuch, a Red Flag Warning is valid for the east Cascades through 10pm \nthis evening. \n\nFriday through Wednesday: The trough over the area will retrograde \nwestward off the coast due as the offshore low moves northward. This \nwill stay in roughly the same place through Tuesday of next week, \nand will result in mostly southwesterly flow through these days. \nInstability continues to ramp up each afternoon and evening due to \nwarming temperatures and continued higher than normal PWATs (120-150 \npercent of normal). Main things to keep an eye on will be any small \nshortwaves that may move through over the weekend. Right now, it \nlooks as though the SE end of Spokane County may experience a quick \nthunderstorm or two on Saturday as a shortwave moves in. On Monday \nthrough Tuesday, a trough will drop down over the state of \nWashington. While much of the convection prior to this will be \nmostly confined to the mountains and ID Panhandle, thunderstorm \nchances become more widespread on these days. Due to impacts \nfrom these upcoming thunderstorms being very similar to those \nseen yesterday and today, we'll continue to monitor each day for\nfire weather concerns associated with lightning and gusty \nwinds. \n\nWednesday through Friday: Cluster analysis and long-term models \nremain in disagreement over whether we will continue seeing a cooler \nand wetter pattern or transition to a warmer and drier pattern. \nRoughly half of the clusters show much of the PNW in a drier and \nwarmer pattern to end next week, while the other half shows us \ncontinuing to see lower heights. This will depend on whether the low \noff the west coast strengthens or dissipates. Overall: expect \nunsettled weather conditions to continue with chances of afternoon \nand evening thunderstorms in mountain areas. Temperatures will \nremain very close to or just below normal through the next week \nwith only Minor HeatRisk expected. /AS",
